<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">LuxLeaks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Luxembourg Leaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">LuxLeaks was a financial scandal revealed in November 2014 by the International Consortium of Investigative Journalists. Leaked documents from the accounting firm PricewaterhouseCoopers showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>'s tax authorities had approved hundreds of advance tax rulings allowing multinational companies to pay very low effective tax rates. The revelations embarrassed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-juncker">Jean-Claude Juncker</a>, prime minister during the period, and prompted EU rules on the exchange of tax rulings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxleaks.jpg" alt="LuxLeaks" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: International Consortium of Investigative Journalists Luxembourg tax authorities · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tax_ruling_luxembourg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Foreign corporations began to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> in large numbers in the early 1990s, after the country transposed an EU directive that let companies pay taxes in a European headquarters country other than where their subsidiaries operated. Tax rulings were drawn up by large accounting firms for their multinational clients and approved by the Luxembourg tax administration. They used devices such as transfer pricing and intragroup loans to move profits into Luxembourg, where they were taxed at very low rates; 1,600 companies were registered at a single address, 5 rue Guillaume Kroll.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 5 November 2014 the International Consortium of Investigative Journalists published its investigation, in which eighty journalists had reviewed 28,000 pages covering 548 tax rulings from 2002 to 2010. A second wave, LuxLeaks 2, followed on 9 December 2014 with the names of about 30 more large companies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-juncker">Jean-Claude Juncker</a> had become president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> days before, and on 27 November 2014 the European Parliament rejected a motion of censure against his Commission. Luxembourg's finance minister, Pierre Gramegna, called the leak the worst attack his country had experienced.</p><p>Three people were charged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>: the former PricewaterhouseCoopers employees Antoine Deltour and Raphaël Halet, and the journalist Edouard Perrin. At the trial in 2016 Deltour received a 12-month suspended sentence and Halet a 9-month one, with fines, and Perrin was acquitted. On appeal in 2017 their sentences were reduced. On 11 January 2018 the Court of Cassation granted Deltour whistleblower status but dismissed Halet's appeal; in February 2023 the Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights ruled that Halet's conviction had violated Article 10 of the European Convention on Human Rights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In October 2015 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> ruled that tax deals for Fiat Finance and Trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and for Starbucks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> were illegal state aid, and Commissioner Margrethe Vestager said the LuxLeaks information had been used. A Tax Transparency Package of 18 March 2015 set up the automatic exchange of advance tax rulings between member states, in force from 1 January 2017. A first Anti-Tax Avoidance Directive followed in 2016 and a second in 2017, a directive on tax intermediaries known as DAC6 in 2018, and a directive protecting whistleblowers, adopted in spring 2019, which cited LuxLeaks among its examples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “LuxLeaks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its tax arrangements for multinational companies drew international criticism, notably in the ‘<b>LuxLeaks</b>’ revelations of 2014, and banking secrecy was largely given up under international pressure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
